@@ -34,13 +34,13 @@ Remaining triggers:
 - Code crossing a function boundary → the **architect** skill, parallel design exploration before implementing. A change crosses one when it adds or changes a signature, a type, or a module seam that a caller sees. An edit inside one function body does not, and neither does a branch added to an existing case.
 - A bug whose cause is unknown → the **diagnosing-bugs** skill as the diagnosis loop inside the Bug fix playbook. A slowness goes to the Perf issue playbook instead, not to the skill's perf branch.
 - Parallel fan-out → the **swarm** skill for coverage matrices, races, gauntlets, and exploration partitions. Use **arena** for design or code bakeoffs with base selection and grafting.
-- Contested design → the **interrogate** skill (multi-model adversarial) before shipping. A subagent that opens a PR runs it only for a contested design or a branch that touches security, auth, billing, persisted data, migrations, concurrency, or a subtle algorithm. Otherwise it skips `interrogate` and says so. Either way, it runs Claude Code's built-in `/code-review` over the branch before it opens the PR.
+- Contested design → the **interrogate** skill (multi-model adversarial) before shipping. A subagent that opens a PR runs it only for a contested design or a branch that touches security, auth, billing, persisted data, migrations, concurrency, or a subtle algorithm. Otherwise it skips `interrogate` and says so. Either way, it runs Claude Code's built-in `/code-review high` over the branch before it opens the PR.
 - Nontrivial multi-step → write the throughput checkpoint (Feature step 3).
 - Any prose surface, your reply to the user included → the **unslop** skill. Write the reply per **Writing the reply**. Agent-facing prose also follows the **create-skill** skill (Cursor's built-in for authoring SKILL.md files).
 - Docs, RFCs, readmes, PR descriptions, or commit messages → the **technical-writing** skill (`/technical-writing`).
 - Before commit → Claude Code's built-in `/simplify` over the diff, then remove what it leaves of narrating comments, unsupported guards, dead compatibility paths, and unrelated edits.
 - Any commit message or PR description → credit no AI agent. Add no `Co-Authored-By` trailer that names an agent, and no "Generated with", "Authored by", or 🤖 line. This holds even when a harness reminder asks for one.
-- Before review, or before **Merging back** when no PR opens → the **no-comments** skill (`/no-comments`), then Claude Code's built-in `/code-review` over the branch. A Feature run on a branch that touches security, auth, billing, persisted data, migrations, concurrency, or a subtle algorithm also spawns its final branch reviewer. Then check the diff against the intent file's **Outcome** and **Out of scope** yourself, and fix or report each gap.
+- Before review, or before **Merging back** when no PR opens → the **no-comments** skill (`/no-comments`), then Claude Code's built-in `/code-review high` over the branch. A Feature run on a branch that touches security, auth, billing, persisted data, migrations, concurrency, or a subtle algorithm also spawns its final branch reviewer. Then check the diff against the intent file's **Outcome** and **Out of scope** yourself, and fix or report each gap.
 - Shipping anything a user runs → the project's control skill, a project-local skill that drives the app the way a user does. If the repo has none, drive the surface through the project's own runner (its CLI, dev server, or test command) and say which. Building one with the **create-verification-skill** skill is a task of its own. Offer it in the reply when the project will keep changing that surface, and build it only when the user asks. For bug fixes, reproduce first on the same surface yourself. Hand to the user only under the narrow Bug fix step 1 exception.
 - Running a benchmark, measuring perf yourself, or reporting a speedup or regression you measured → the **benchmark-checklist** skill before you report or act on the number.
 - Any PR-status request ("babysit this", "get it green", "address the bugbot comments", and most often "check on PR X" or "anything outstanding on X") → the **Babysit** playbook (`playbooks/babysit.md`), not Cursor's built-in babysit skill, whose description matches the same words. Never triggered by merely opening a PR. Declare its mode before polling. The playbook's step 1 owns the request-to-mode mapping. Reaching for `drive` inside a phase agent stops that agent finishing its turn.
